Stockroom — Inventory Management System

A lightweight, browser-based inventory management application built with HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Stockroom helps users track products, monitor stock levels, and manage inventory through a simple, responsive interface.

Features

Inventory Management: Add, edit, and delete inventory items.
Stock Control: Reduce item quantities and monitor remaining stock.
Search and Filtering: Find products and filter inventory by stock status.
Dashboard: View inventory summary counts.
Data Persistence: Save inventory in the browser using localStorage.
CSV Export: Export inventory data for reporting and recordkeeping.
SKU Validation: Prevent duplicate stock-keeping units.
Responsive Interface: Use the application on desktop and mobile screens.
Technologies Used

HTML5
CSS3
JavaScript (Vanilla JavaScript)
Browser localStorage
CSV file export
Getting Started

Run Locally

Clone this repository:
Navigate to the project folder:
Open index.html in your browser, or open the project in Visual Studio Code and use a local development server.
No backend server or database installation is required for the current version.

How to Use

Add an inventory item with its product details and quantity.
Edit product information as needed.
Reduce stock when inventory is used or sold.
Search for products or filter items by stock status.
Delete items that are no longer needed.
Export inventory data as a CSV file.
Refresh the page to confirm that your saved inventory remains available in the same browser.
Data Storage and Limitations

Stockroom currently stores inventory data in the browser’s localStorage.

Data is stored separately in each browser and on each device.
Inventory is not shared between different users.
Clearing browser storage may remove saved inventory.
The current version does not include user accounts, cloud synchronization, or a server-side database.
This version is intended as a functional prototype and demonstration of browser-based inventory management.

Future Improvements

Potential enhancements include:

Backend API and database integration
User authentication and role-based permissions
Shared inventory across multiple devices
Low-stock alerts
Inventory transaction history
Automated testing and deployment improvements
Project Purpose

This project demonstrates practical application development using core web technologies, including DOM manipulation, event handling, functions, arrays and objects, form validation, browser storage, filtering, and data export.

It is part of an ongoing effort to build practical software solutions and strengthen full-stack development skills.

